<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n38458" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">prōcĭdŭus</span></b> ,
			a, um, adj.
		1. procido. <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>Fallen down</i>, <i>prostrate</i>
			(post-Aug.): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">salix procidua,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Plin.
					16, 32, 57, § 133</span></i>
			; <i><span class="en">Stat. Th. 3, 127</span></i>;
				<i><span class="en">Sid. Carm. 23,
			415</span></i>.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			Of parts of the body, <i>falling down</i> or <i>forwards</i>, <i>prociduous</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">sedes,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Plin.
					21, 20, 87, § 151</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">umbilicus,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">id.
					20, 20, 81, § 211</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
